      {
        "heading": "5 EVALUATION",
        "text": "In this section, we first compare SABR to existing certified training methods before investigating its behavior in an ablation study.\nExperimental Setup We implement SABR in PyTorch (Paszke et al., 2019) and use MN-BAB (Ferrari et al., 2022) for certification. We conduct experiments on MNIST (LeCun et al., 2010), CIFAR-10 (Krizhevsky et al., 2009), and TINYIMAGENET (Le & Yang, 2015) for the challenging `\u221e perturbations, using the same 7-layer convolutional architecture CNN7 as prior work (Shi et al., 2021) unless indicated otherwise (see App. B for more details). We choose similar training hyperparameters as prior work (Shi et al., 2021) and provide more detailed information in App. B.\n5.1 MAIN RESULTS\nStd. Acc.\nCert. Acc.\nWe compare SABR to state-of-the-art certified training methods in Table 1 and Fig. 6, reporting the best results achieved with a given method on any architecture.\nIn Fig. 6, we show certified over standard accuracy (upper right-hand corner is best) and observe that SABR ( ) dominates all other methods, achieving both the highest certified and standard accuracy across all settings. As existing methods typically perform well either at large or small perturbation radii (see Table 1 and Fig. 6), we believe the high performance of SABR across perturbation radii to be particularly promising.\nMethods striving to balance accuracy and regularization by bridging the gap between provable and adversarial training ( , )(Balunovic & Vechev, 2020; Palma et al., 2022) perform only slightly worse than SABR at small perturbation radii (CIFAR-10 = 2/255), but much worse at large radii, e.g., attaining only 27.5% ( ) and 27.9% ( ) certifiable accuracy for CIFAR-10 = 8/255 compared to 35.25% ( ). Similarly, methods focusing purely on certi-\nfied accuracy by directly optimizing over-approximations of the worst-case loss ( , ) (Gowal et al., 2018b; Zhang et al., 2020) tend to perform well at large perturbation radii (MNIST = 0.3 and CIFAR-10 = 8/255), but poorly at small perturbation radii, e.g. on CIFAR-10 at = 2/255, SABR improves natural accuracy to 79.5% ( ) up from 66.8% ( ) and 71.5% ( ) and even more significantly certified accuracy to 62.6% ( ) up from 52.9% ( ) and 54.0% ( ). On the particularly challenging TINYIMAGENET, SABR again dominates all existing certified training methods, improving certified and standard accuracy by almost 3%.\nTo summarize, SABR improves strictly on all existing certified training methods across all commonly used benchmarks with relative improvements exceeding 25% in some cases.\nIn contrast to certified training methods Zhang et al. (2021) propose a new architecture with inherent `\u221e-robustness properties. While these networks attain higher certified accuracies on CIFAR-10 at = 8/255, their training is notoriously hard (Zhang et al., 2021; 2022a), yielding low standard accuracies of, e.g., only 60.6% compared to 79.52% for CIFAR-10 at = 2/255. Further robustness can only be obtained against one perturbation type at a time."
